  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nUnsettled weather will continue through early next week as the large-\nscale trough that will settle across the west coast over the weekend \nwill gradually migrate eastward. The associated large-scale forcing \nfor ascent combined with ample low-level moisture will support \nscattered showers and embedded thunderstorms during the day, mainly \nacross the foothills and higher terrain areas north and east of \nPhoenix in upslope flow. Thereafter, as the main cold front \nassociated with the trough moves through late Monday night into \nTuesday morning, there will likely be a band of showers with \nembedded thunderstorms that moves through, particularly across south-\ncentral AZ, before rain chances end by Tuesday afternoon/evening as \ndrier air filters in. At this point any of the rain that falls early \nnext week will generally be of the nuisance type. Quieter weather\nwith near zero rain chances can be expected starting Wednesday \nand will persist through the end of the week.\n\nWith the presence of the trough as well as more abundant cloud \ncover, well-below normal temperatures are expected during the first \nhalf of the week with many of the lower desert locations likely \nstaying in the 70s for afternoon highs. Early morning lows will also \nbe below normal with the coolest morning expected on Wednesday when \nmany areas are expected to drop into the 50s with even some 40s \nacross the higher terrain areas. Temperatures will then gradually \nrebound into the 80s by the latter portion of the week.",
